#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115070 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/a5/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/48/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/81/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115071 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/f4/f4478e5828da7c79a47bf3ca212ed15357befe7b0b208c78beb0895b445272c9.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](images/cf/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115124 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173" :question/problem "Given the system\n![](images/42/427c19efbebba6bfe2dc36bccd852d7a1f5e0ec97dc46c00043d019807e86ec2.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/50/50f13deaa03c2af30f511ef3d514e3dd2e118d81d9b121b436ff1a2f2497c303.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/e0/e02bb5004f792eea20216e64098bb924dbed15defb9330967d6ae5a144e067dc.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3ccae38-2121-52d3-adbe-774101358f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402011} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1763ae-5fcd-5d4b-b18a-af3a643749d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402012} {:answer/id #uuid "300bc792-b634-5ba3-8561-858fdf7d8d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402013} {:answer/id #uuid "55911223-4948-5180-95bf-96b38f95603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-6{x}_{3} \\\\ 10+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402014} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a39045-c764-56cd-a094-6caa0b024ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ccae38-2121-52d3-adbe-774101358f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402011} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d6271a-24bd-58c0-9553-83b2dc10742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f65d67ed-b94b-50cc-87d4-9aa7130f7547" :question/math-academy-id "q-32247" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f9fc63-1e5b-58f3-9165-48ecf337098a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324140} {:answer/id #uuid "6531f6ef-7836-5c36-8ad2-b64340ef2dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324806} {:answer/id #uuid "716e8f1f-3359-5b9c-a2b5-8043eda079e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324907} {:answer/id #uuid "942d2637-f186-5cc3-808c-b7d930c541bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325234} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ddd36b-c315-54de-90f4-9ca1832ae646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "942d2637-f186-5cc3-808c-b7d930c541bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325234} :answer-field/id #uuid "715194d1-fad4-5aa4-83e8-d5906bffa1a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e80c83a9-9107-528b-af57-73287d15e8e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42061" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y+z=0 \\\\  & -4x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we can to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01308e53-b92a-5d41-8e41-3edcada2f882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342256} {:answer/id #uuid "1c251f13-a18e-56fb-b700-41d13ecaa3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342477} {:answer/id #uuid "b0020fb9-0863-546c-95a8-236e93a1dd5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343785} {:answer/id #uuid "c309108b-fd3a-5760-8a95-009d5ec4b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343955} {:answer/id #uuid "d75038c3-3bd3-5a22-8af1-6c84cbbc8269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c251f13-a18e-56fb-b700-41d13ecaa3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342477} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cccc34-d274-5062-9c39-f9e811dad04c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0089d19-a4b5-5ec4-beaa-917a294458ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-74637" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+3y=-1 \\\\  & x-2y=1 \\\\  & -x-5y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=1 \\\\ 7y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b9a3770-af37-58af-91e7-3430fd2463b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343154} {:answer/id #uuid "71cae964-c567-5585-ab23-7241fb114732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343221} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f3da90-4b17-5b45-af78-2746f79a0b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343724} {:answer/id #uuid "ad97781e-f202-56a0-803a-f17ee1a49ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343764} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d5b11f-4138-5c57-8bc8-f9f3a404cfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d5b11f-4138-5c57-8bc8-f9f3a404cfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344356} :answer-field/id #uuid "6517af05-6089-578f-972a-9310f8010c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0c28bd7-70de-59aa-93c9-933cbaca4593" :question/math-academy-id "q-74646" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=2 \\\\  & 2x+(k+1)y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=2 \\\\ (k-7)y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$"}]}
